## sevenz-rust2

`crates/cash-archive/src/sevenz`, the 7z format that `7z` and `7za` read and write, is
taken from [sevenz-rust2](https://github.com/hasenbanck/sevenz-rust2) 0.23.0, itself a
fork of [sevenz-rust](https://github.com/dyz1990/sevenz-rust), © their authors, used
under the Apache License 2.0, whose text is in
[licenses/sevenz-rust2-Apache-2.0.txt](licenses/sevenz-rust2-Apache-2.0.txt). Every file
of the module came from it and has been modified.

Changes: the reader hands out entries in the archive's order with their own CRC checks
and 7-Zip's kinds of data problems, maps entries without data that stand between a
block's entries, reads the facts 7-Zip lists (physical and headers size, encrypted
headers, the start header's version) and copies packed blocks raw; the writer lays the
header out as 7-Zip does (alignment, property order, no packed-stream CRCs), copies
blocks from another archive, takes a block's entries one by one as they are read (so a
file that will not open is left out), ends coder chains explicitly, rounds the LZMA2
dictionary property up, and writes AES as 7-Zip does (2^19 rounds, no salt); Deflate64
is read; brotli, LZ4, zstd, the WebAssembly parts and the file-walking helpers are left
out.

## rars

`crates/cash-archive/src/rar`, RAR archives read (RAR 1.3 to 7, encrypted too) and written
(RAR 2.9 and 5/7, with recovery records), is taken from
[rars](https://github.com/bitplane/rars) 0.10.0, © its authors, used under the Apache
License 2.0, whose text is in [licenses/rars-Apache-2.0.txt](licenses/rars-Apache-2.0.txt).
Every file of the module came from it, and its tests and their archives are in
`crates/cash-archive/tests/rar` and `tests/fixtures/rar`.

Changes: it is a module of cash-archive, whose features carry its own (`write`,
`parallel`, `recovery`, `encryption`); passwords and keys are cleared by a small wipe of
its own where it used the `zeroize` crate (the AES key schedules are not); what a damaged
archive can reach where it called `unwrap` or `expect` is an error, a poisoned lock gives
its data back, and the rest says why it cannot fail; a FILETIME is formatted from its
count, as Windows cannot hold every one in a `SystemTime`; a header cipher is cached with
its salt only once it is made.
